    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-12468",
    ":question/problem": "If $x = y + 2y\\sqrt y$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$ at the point $\\left(3 , 1\\right).$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2{y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =1+3{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =1+3\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} =  \\dfrac{1}{1 + 3\\sqrt y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,1)} & =\\frac{1}{1+3\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
