      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-32595",
      ":question/problem": "If $x=2\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(5,9).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{1/2})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1/2}+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}+\\frac{3}{2y\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+3}{2y\\sqrt{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{2y\\sqrt{y}}{2y+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(5,9),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,9)} & =\\frac{2(9)\\sqrt{9}}{2(9)+3}=\\frac{18}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
