      ":question/problem": "If $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}+1,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(3,8).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt[3]{y}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt[3]{y})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{1/3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{-2/3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{y}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,8),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,8)} & =3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
