      ":question/problem": "If $x=6\\sqrt[3]{y}+1,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(13,8).$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(13,8)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(6\\sqrt[3]{y}+1) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt[3]{y})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(1) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{1/3})+0 \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{y}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(13,8),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(13,8)} & =\\frac{{(\\sqrt[3]{8})}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
