      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-32662",
      ":question/problem": "If $x=y\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(8,4).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y\\sqrt{y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}-y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}-y+2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(8,4),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(8,4)} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{4}-4+2)}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
