      ":question/problem": "If $x=2{y}^{3}+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(20,2).$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(20,2)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3{y}^{2})+2y \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6{y}^{2}+2y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(20,2),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(20,2)} & =\\frac{1}{6({2}^{2})+2(2)}=\\frac{1}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
