      ":question/id": "0a20dd68-509a-5704-9c10-a08b87646850",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-341745",
      ":question/problem": "If $x={y}^{4}-\\frac{3}{y},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(4,-1).$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,-1)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4}-\\frac{3}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-3(-{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+3{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{5}+3}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4{y}^{5}+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(4,-1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,-1)} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{5}+3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
